\v 19 Then¦119746 Yahweh's¦119749 spirit¦119748 \add ≈rose up in\add* him¦119747, and¦119750 he¦119750 went to Ashkelon¦119751 and¦119752 killed¦119752 thirty¦119755 men¦119756 \add there\add*. He¦119752 took¦119757 their¦119760 possessions, and¦119761 he¦119761 gave¦119761 the¦119762 changes¦119762 of¦119763 clothes for the¦119764 riddle¦119764. \add ≈But¦119746 he¦119757 was very angry¦119766, so\add* he went \add ≈back to his¦119766 parent's home¦119768\add*.
\v 20 Then \add (unknown to Shimshon)\add*, \add *his\add* wife¦119773 \add ≈was¦119777 given to the¦119773 man who'd been¦119777\add* his close¦119775 friend¦119777 and¦119772 companion¦119775.
\c 15
\rem /s1 Samson's Vengeance on the Philistines; Samson Defeats the Philistines
\p
\v 1 Some days¦119781 later during the wheat¦119785 harvest¦119783, Shimshon¦119787 visited¦119786 his¦119790 wife¦119790 with¦119791 a young¦119791 goat¦119791 \add as a present for the family, because\add* he¦119793 said¦119793, “I'll go¦119794 into¦119795 the¦119798 bedroom¦119798 with my¦119797 wife.” \add ≈But¦119780\add* her¦119802 father¦119802 wouldn't allow¦119794 him¦119801 to¦119798 go in¦119782,
\v 2 and¦119805 \add ≈pleaded\add*, “Truly, I \add ≈I thought¦119808 that¦119809 you must\add* hate¦119811 her¦119812, so I gave¦119813 her¦119813 to¦119814 your¦119814 close¦119814 friend¦119814. \add ≈But¦119805 wouldn't\add* her¦119816 younger¦119817 sister¦119816 be¦119816 better¦119818 \add anyway\add*? Please¦119822, \add ≈would you take her instead¦119824\add*.”
