\v 18 Then¦120104 he¦120104 was¦120104 very¦120105 thirsty¦120104 and¦120106 cried¦120106 out to¦120107 Yahweh¦120109, “You¦120111 yourself have¦120112 given¦120112 your¦120115 servant¦120115 this great¦120119 victory¦120118, \add ≈but¦120104\add* now¦120121 must¦120122 I¦120122 die¦120122 with¦120113 this¦120120 thirst¦120123 and \add ≈get captured by¦120113 those\add* uncircumcised¦120126 \add Philistines\add*?”
\v 19 \add ≈So¦120128\add* God¦120129 split¦120128 open¦120128 the¦120132 \add little\add* hollow¦120132 that's¦120133 at¦120135 Lehi¦120135, and¦120136 water¦120138 came¦120136 out of it. When¦120128 he drank¦120139, his¦120141 \add ≈strength\add* returned¦120140 and¦120142 he¦120142 revived¦120142. Because of that¦120155, he called¦120147 it ‘Eyn-Hakkore¦120150’ \add (which means ‘the fountain of the one who called out’)\add*, which is¦120135 in¦120135 Lehi¦120152 to this¦120155 day¦120154.
\v 20 And¦120157 \add so\add* \add @Shimshon¦120157\add* led¦120157 Yisrael¦120160 for twenty¦120163 years¦120164 at¦120161 the¦120161 time¦120161 of¦120161 the¦120162 Philistine¦120162 \add oppression\add*.
\c 16
\s1 Shimshon carries away the gates
